---
/**
 * WU18 — the image → dot-scene laboratory surface.
 *
 * The user asked how an image becomes this style and supplied a film-prop test
 * input. This surface demonstrates the answer with drawings WE own: each input
 * is converted by the same pipeline the CLI runs (`scripts/scene-src/from-image.mjs`)
 * in this page's frontmatter, and the CONVERTED scene is embedded as data, so the
 * live canvas draws the same conversion the Node raster produced — not a second
 * one. The third-party gist is evidence only: it is never embedded here, never
 * committed and never shipped, and the note below says so.
 *
 * It is a STILL by default (the poster frame) so a comparison does not move while
 * it is read; Play animates the views. Every pane is decorative and hidden from
 * assistive technology, and every pane carries a written description so the page
 * is honest without JavaScript.
 */
import { LAB_TREATMENTS } from '../../../scripts/scene-src/local-inputs.mjs';
import { DELIVERY_CELL } from '../../../scripts/scene-src/engine.mjs';

interface ConvertedInput {
  id: string;
  label: string;
  licence: string;
  cols: number;
  rows: number;
  payload: string;
  stats: { inkedCells: number; inkedFraction: number; meanLevel: number; fit: { scale: number; drawnWidth: number; drawnHeight: number; margin: number } };
  palette: string[];
}
interface Props {
  inputs: ConvertedInput[];
  /** WU20: the film-prop demonstration, converted at build time when the
   * gitignored local fixture exists on the build machine; null otherwise. */
  demonstration?: ConvertedInput | null;
  cost: string;
  defaultInput: string;
}
const { inputs, demonstration = null, cost, defaultInput } = Astro.props;
const allInputs = demonstration ? [...inputs, demonstration] : inputs;
const first = inputs.find((entry) => entry.id === defaultInput) ?? inputs[0];
const selectClass = 'mt-1 w-full border border-hairline bg-ink-950 px-2 py-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-bone-100 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent';
const controlLabelClass = 'flex flex-col font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-plomo-400';
const views = [
  { cell: DELIVERY_CELL, width: 'w-full', note: `${DELIVERY_CELL} px per cell, drawn at the column width` },
  { cell: DELIVERY_CELL, width: 'w-80 max-w-full', note: `${DELIVERY_CELL} px per cell, drawn at 320 px` },
  { cell: 4, width: 'w-full', note: '4 px per cell, shown at the same column width — the small-pitch case' },
];
---
<section data-image-lab data-image-scene={first.id} class="border-t border-hairline py-8" aria-labelledby="image-title">
  <h2 id="image-title" class="text-xl text-bone-50">04 / Image study · an image becomes a scene</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
    Each drawing below is a local input of ours, rasterised, fitted to the grid with its aspect preserved inside a
    declared margin, measured per cell (ink coverage for line art, luminance for a photograph) and handed to the
    engine as a field — so the measured coverage ladder, the dither and the per-cell tone compensation are the
    engine's own, not a second implementation. The converted scene is embedded as data and the canvas draws
    <span class="text-bone-100">that same conversion</span>, which is why the Node raster and this canvas hash
    identically. The overlays are periodic by construction: every drift travels a whole number of lattice wraps per
    loop, and the seam is measured on the field, never on a frame index. It is a still at the poster frame; Play
    animates the views.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">{cost}</p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
    <span class="text-bone-100">Rights and the film-prop demonstration (WU20).</span> The user's probe input — a
    Blade Runner unicorn — is a film prop design, attributed to Tom Southwell. At the user's explicit direction it is
    shown here as a demonstration, converted at build time from a local, gitignored fixture: the SVG itself is never
    committed, never copied into <span class="font-mono">src/</span> or <span class="font-mono">public/</span>, and
    only the converted dot-scene serialisation is embedded on this noindex laboratory page. Production use requires
    confirmed rights; until then it is a demonstration, not an asset. When the local fixture is absent, the
    demonstration is documented instead of shipped, and you can reproduce it locally with the same CLI as any other
    input:
    <span class="mt-1 block break-all font-mono text-xs text-bone-100">node scripts/scene-src/from-image.mjs --in your-local-copy.svg --mode ink --cols 160 --rows 80 --out dist-motion-verify/wu20 --png</span>
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
    <span class="text-bone-100">Convert your own file.</span> The same pipeline runs from the command line on any local
    SVG, PNG or JPEG — no upload, no network:
    <span class="mt-1 block break-all font-mono text-xs text-bone-100">node scripts/scene-src/from-image.mjs --in your-file.svg --mode ink --color levels --palette ink --overlays stars,aurora --out dist-motion-verify/wu19 --png</span>
    Add <span class="font-mono">--prefilter none</span> to disable the anti-moiré prefilter, or
    <span class="font-mono">--auto-gain none</span> to disable the per-image normalisation; both are on by default.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
    <span class="text-bone-100">Quality, measured (WU19).</span> A fine diagonal hatch in our calibration target used to
    alias into a dominant dither block: the hatch beat against the cell lattice (tone coefficient of variation 0.8145
    across the hatch region). Conversions now pass the source through a prefilter matched to the cell pitch before
    measuring, which averages sub-lattice detail into tone (hatch variation 0.3519 with the region's mean tone kept
    within 0.4%); the trade-off is that strokes narrower than a cell soften slightly. Sparse light drawings used to
    land almost entirely in the empty step; an auto-gain now normalises each image (gain recorded in the scene;
    1.0 for full-range art, and a mild gain for dark art such as our mark, whose prefiltered ink p90 is 0.6009). The
    ladder gained a fifth rung at full coverage, so highlights can
    close a cell completely. What did NOT improve is stated in <span class="font-mono">docs/AUDITORIA-ESCENAS.md</span>.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
    <label class={controlLabelClass}>
      input
      <select data-image-control="input" class={selectClass}>
        {allInputs.map((entry) => <option value={entry.id} selected={entry.id === first.id}>{entry.label}</option>)}
      </select>
    </label>
    <label class={controlLabelClass}>
      treatment
      <select data-image-control="treatment" class={selectClass}>
        {LAB_TREATMENTS.map((entry) => (
          <option value={entry.id} data-palette={entry.palette} data-overlays={entry.overlays.join(',')}>
            {entry.label}
          </option>
        ))}
      </select>
    </label>
    <label class={controlLabelClass}>
      grid
      <select data-image-control="density" class={selectClass}>
        <option value="1">{first.cols} × {first.rows} cells — the converted grid</option>
        <option value="2">{Math.round(first.cols / 2)} × {Math.round(first.rows / 2)} cells</option>
        <option value="4">{Math.round(first.cols / 4)} × {Math.round(first.rows / 4)} cells</option>
      </select>
    </label>
  </div>
  <p data-image-readout class="mt-4 font-mono text-xs text-plomo-400" role="status">
    Static fallback · the image study needs JavaScript to switch inputs and treatments.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-3">
    <button type="button" data-image-toggle disabled aria-pressed="false" class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-accent disabled:opacity-60">
      Play
    </button>
    <p data-image-status class="font-mono text-xs text-plomo-400" role="status">Static · JavaScript unavailable</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
    {
      views.map((view) => (
        <div class={view.width}>
          <div class="scene-frame bg-ink-900">
            <canvas
              data-image-view
              data-image-cell={view.cell}
              width={first.cols * view.cell}
              height={first.rows * view.cell}
              aria-hidden="true"
            />
            <p data-image-fallback class="p-4 text-sm text-plomo-400">
              {`${first.label}, converted to ${first.cols} × ${first.rows} cells and drawn at ${view.cell} px per cell. The live frame needs JavaScript.`}
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-plomo-400">{view.note}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-plomo-400">
    Measured for the default input (<span class="font-mono">{first.id}</span>): fitted scale
    <span class="font-mono">{first.stats.fit.scale.toFixed(6)}</span> into
    <span class="font-mono">{first.stats.fit.drawnWidth.toFixed(1)} × {first.stats.fit.drawnHeight.toFixed(1)}</span>
    cells inside a <span class="font-mono">{(first.stats.fit.margin * 100).toFixed(0)}%</span> margin;
    <span class="font-mono">{first.stats.inkedCells}</span> inked cells of
    <span class="font-mono">{first.cols * first.rows}</span>
    (fraction <span class="font-mono">{first.stats.inkedFraction.toFixed(4)}</span>); palette depth
    <span class="font-mono">{first.palette.length}</span> with the ground at index 0. Those are measurements of this
    conversion, not estimates.
  </p>
  {
    allInputs.map((entry) => (
      <script is:inline type="application/json" data-image-scene={entry.id} set:html={entry.payload} />
    ))
  }
</section>

<script>
  import '~/lib/scene-runtime';
</script>

<style>
  .scene-frame {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  [data-image-lab] canvas {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  [data-image-lab]:not([data-ready]) canvas {
    display: none;
  }
  [data-image-lab][data-ready] [data-image-fallback] {
    display: none;
  }
  [data-image-lab][data-ready] [data-image-readout] {
    min-height: 1rem;
  }
</style>
